.TH QUANUX-QXP 7 "January 2026" "QuanuX" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
quanux-qxp \- QuanuX Extension Protocol
.SH DESCRIPTION
The **QuanuX Extension Protocol (QXP)** is the architectural standard for extending the QuanuX platform. Unlike traditional plugin systems that run code *inside* the main process, QXP uses **Sidecar Processes**.

.SH PHILOSOPHY
"Bolt-on, don't Build-in."

1. **Isolation**: Extensions run as separate OS processes. A crash in an extension never brings down the core.
2. **Polyglot**: While **Go** is the standard, extensions can technically be written in any language that supports standard I/O and HTTP/WebSocket.
3. **Security**: Extensions operate with specific permissions and have no implicit access to the Core's memory or database.

.SH DIRECTORY STRUCTURE
All extensions reside in \fI/extensions\fR.
.P
.nf
/extensions
  /rithmic
    extension.yaml   (Manifest)
    main.go          (Entrypoint)
    /proto           (Protocol Buffers)
    README.md
.fi

.SH THE MANIFEST
Every extension is defined by an \fIextension.yaml\fR file.
.P
.nf
name: "rithmic"
version: "1.0.0"
runtime: "go"
command: "go run main.go"
permissions:
  - "market.data.read"
env:
  - "QUANUX_RITHMIC_USER"
.fi

.SH DATA FLOW
.SS 1. Control Plane (HTTP/gRPC/Stdio)
QuanuX Core manages the extension process (Start/Stop). It injects configuration via Environment Variables.

.SS 2. Data Plane (ZeroMQ/NATS)
High-volume market data (ticks, depth) is NOT sent back to Core via standard HTTP. Instead, extensions publish to a high-performance bus (ZeroMQ or NATS) which the Core subscribes to.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quanuxctl (1),
.BR quanuxctl-ext (1)
